## 3. Quick start

1. Copy the folder to the machine running Isaac Sim.
2. In `run_inspection.py`, set `SCRIPT_DIR` to that folder. This is the only path to set: the engine and the
   kinematic model are found next to `borescope_config.py`.
3. In Isaac Sim: **Window > Script Editor**, open `run_inspection.py`, press **Run**.
4. Run it again at any time. Every run stops the previous one and reopens the engine, so each run starts clean.

What happens after Run:

| Time after Play | What the probe does |
|---|---|
| 0 – 1.05 s | Joint-direction self-check: one joint is bent at a time to measure PhysX's sign conventions. |
| 1.05 – 1.6 s | Settles at the first pose of the profile (`PROBE_HOLD_S`). |
| 1.6 s → end | Plays the motion profile. IMU / ROS 2 publishing runs during this phase only (stamps start at 0). |

## 4. Viewing (important for the web viewer)

Two views open automatically:

- **Main viewport: tip camera** (`/PortProbe/Head/TipCamera`). The scene is lit by the probe's LED only. If it
  is too dark, use **Lighting > Stage Lights** in that viewport, or raise `LED_GAIN` (4 – 10).
- **"Probe observer" window: third-person view** of the probe inside the engine. The inside of the engine is
  dark, so in this window select **Lighting > Camera Light**. This has to be done by hand once per session:
  the setting is not enabled from code because it would also light the tip camera and change the published
  images.

Tips for the web viewer (streamed video):

- The stream lowers its quality when Isaac renders slowly. If the image is blurry, reload the browser tab.
- Rendering cost: the main viewport, the observer window and the 1280×1024 ROS camera feed are rendered every
  frame. To speed it up, close the observer window (`SECOND_VIEWPORT = False`), publish smaller images
  (`ROS_IMAGE_RES = (640, 512)`), or turn the IMU off (`RUN_IMU = False`).
- The probe motion and the IMU run on simulation time (every physics step), so a slow viewer does not change
  the motion or the data.

## 5. How the probe is modelled

The probe is a PhysX articulation (`/PortProbe`) placed on the real HPC port of the engine model:

```
world --fixed--> PortBase --2-axis PIVOT at the port--> Pivot --prismatic PUSH (λ)--> Carriage
      --revolute ROLL (β)--> Tube links --> Neck links --> Head (tip camera + LED)
```

| Part | Model |
|---|---|
| Insertion tube | Rigid capsule links joined by passive 2-axis bending springs (stiffness from the bench-validated `EI_OVER_W_M3`). |
| Bending neck | `NECK_SEGMENTS` capsule links joined by driven 2-axis joints (σ = `NECK_MM`). |
| Head | Rigid head (`HEAD_MM`) carrying the tip camera, the LED and the IMU. |
| Port | The port frame is fitted to the port prim; the probe starts just past the casing's inner wall (`PORT_CLEARANCE_MM`). A visual guide tube passes through the wall. |

The probe is pushed through the port by the insertion joint; it is not teleported. Gravity is not applied to the
probe (PhysX gravity on this light chain is unstable), so the probe follows the kinematics.

## 6. Acceleration-driven motion

### Variables

μ = [θ, α, λ, β]:

| | Meaning | Note |
|---|---|---|
| θ | Bending angle | θ = π − σ/ρ, so θ = π (180°) is a straight neck. |
| α | Rotation of the bending plane about +Y | α = 0 bends toward +Z. |
| λ | Insertion (unsupported length, port → neck start) | Along +Y. |
| β | Roll of the probe about +Y | |

### Integration

The input is the acceleration μ̈(t). It is integrated with an exact double integrator (acceleration constant
over each step `ACCEL_DT`):

```
μ̇[k+1] = μ̇[k] + dt · u[k]
μ[k+1] = μ[k] + dt · μ̇[k] + ½ dt² · u[k]
```

Box constraints on μ, μ̇ and μ̈ (`ACCEL_BOUNDS`, in deg / mm) are enforced at every step:

- acceleration outside its bounds is clipped;
- velocity outside its bounds is clipped (the acceleration is reduced accordingly);
- a position bound is a hard stop (velocity set to zero).

Every event is logged (`[accel] ... limit reached, clipped`); `no box constraint was hit` means the motion is
unconstrained.

### Positions: h₁

The integrated μ(t) is turned into the neck arc, neck end and camera position with the kinematic model,
r(t) = h₁(p, μ(t)) with p = [l; σ] (`ACCEL_H1 = "model"`). The model only evaluates whole trajectories, so h₁ is
computed by building a constant profile at μ and reading its first sample (`accel_profile.model_fk`), at 200
points of the motion, interpolated in between. `ACCEL_H1 = "builtin"` uses a built-in constant-curvature model
instead (faster, but it does not include the model's pivot at the port).

### Where the accelerations come from (`ACCEL_PATH`)

| Value | Accelerations |
|---|---|
| `"profile"` (default) | Reproduce the kinematic model's `PROFILE` exactly. μ(t) is recovered from the profile's positions (at each sample, the μ whose h₁ gives the same neck start, neck end and camera), a cubic spline is fitted per component, and its second derivative is the acceleration. If the speed / acceleration bounds would be exceeded, time is stretched uniformly: same path, slower. |
| `"straight"` | From the profile's initial μ to its final μ, all four on one smooth time law (zero speed and acceleration at both ends). |
| `"segments"` | Predefined test moves (`accel_profile.EXAMPLE_SEGMENTS`): piecewise-constant accelerations. |

Notes on `"profile"`:

- **Roll:** if the model's positions do not depend on β, β cannot be recovered from them; it then follows the
  insertion progress, β = β₀ + (β₁ − β₀)·s.
- **Alpha while straight:** α has no effect while the neck is straight, so there it is interpolated from the
  bent samples.
- **Start-up time:** recovery evaluates the model a few hundred times (about 1 min with the inspection profile).

### Joint targets

At every physics step, μ(t) is converted into joint targets: insertion, roll, port pivot, and the neck joint
angles that put every neck link on the model's neck arc and the head on the model's camera. PhysX drives the
joints to these targets.

## 7. Camera, IMU and ROS 2

**Tip camera:** calibrated pinhole camera with OpenCV distortion (`CAM_RES`, `FX`, `FY`, `CX`, `CY`, `DIST`),
`CAMERA_OFFSET_MM` ahead of the head's front face, on the probe axis.

**IMU:** with `IMU_AT_CAMERA = True` the IMU coincides with the camera (same position and axes,
`T_imu_cam` = identity). Specific force and angular velocity are computed from the head's simulated pose at every
physics step (central differences); gravity is added to the accelerometer (`READ_GRAVITY_IN_IMU`). Optional white
noise: `ACC_NOISE_STD`, `GYRO_NOISE_STD`.

**Topics** (node `ROS_NODE_NAME`):

| Topic | Type | Rate | Frame id | QoS |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `/imu0` | `sensor_msgs/Imu` | `PHYSICS_HZ` (200 Hz) | `imu0` | best effort |
| `/cam0/image_raw` | `sensor_msgs/Image` (rgb8) | `ROS_CAMERA_HZ` (30 Hz) | `cam0` | best effort |
| `/cam0/camera_info` | `sensor_msgs/CameraInfo` (K, plumb_bob D) | with every image | `cam0` | best effort |
| `/ground_truth_pose` | `geometry_msgs/PoseStamped` | `PHYSICS_HZ` | `world` | reliable |

Check while the profile plays: `ros2 topic list`, `ros2 topic hz /imu0`, `ros2 topic echo /ground_truth_pose`.

**Camera poses for SLAM** (Python, any Script Editor tab): `probe_get_poses()` returns times and 4×4 poses
(`T_port`, `T_world`, OpenCV camera axes); `POSES_NPZ` saves them at the end of the run.

## 8. Frames

**Port frame** (used by the kinematic model, the ground truth with `GT_FRAME = "port"`, and `T_port`):

- origin on the port axis, just past the casing's inner wall;
- +Y along the port, into the engine;
- +Z as close to the stage's up direction as the port allows;
- X = Y × Z.

The port is not exactly level, so gravity in this frame is not exactly (0, 0, −9.81). The run prints its value
in the IMU block.

**Camera:** OpenCV axes (x right, y down, z forward). Ground-truth orientation is published as a quaternion in
ROS order (x, y, z, w).

## 9. Output and validation

Before Play:

```
[accel] profile mu(t) recovered at 61 samples (..), worst position mismatch 0.000x mm
[probe] acceleration path vs 'inspection' profile: camera max 0.09 mm apart
[probe] clearance: closest approach 10.3 mm (probe surface to engine, ...)
```

At the end of the profile:

```
================ JOINTED PROBE vs MODEL ================
neck end : max ... mm   rms ... mm   final ... mm
camera   : max ... mm   rms ... mm   final ... mm
final viewing direction (port frame x, y, z): probe [...] | model [...] | 1.0 deg apart
```

| Check | Meaning | Typical (inspection profile) |
|---|---|---|
| Acceleration path vs profile | Acceleration-driven path vs the model's profile | < 0.1 mm |
| Clearance | Closest approach of the planned probe to the engine; warning below `CLEARANCE_WARN_MM` | 10 mm |
| Probe vs model, final | Simulated probe vs the kinematic model at the end of the motion | ≈ 1 mm |
| Viewing direction | Simulated camera direction vs the model | ≈ 1° |
| IMU block | Message counts and camera errors at the end | 2000 IMU / GT, 0 camera errors (10 s at 200 Hz) |

`python accel_check.py` verifies the integration itself (errors of thousandths of a mm that shrink about 4× when
dt halves).

## 10. Main settings (`borescope_config.py`)

| Setting | What it changes |
|---|---|
| `PROFILE`, `SOURCE`, `ACCEL_PATH` | What motion is played. |
| `ACCEL_BOUNDS` | Box constraints on position, speed and acceleration. |
| `PHYSICS_HZ` | Physics and IMU rate. |
| `NECK_DRIVE`, `PUSH_DRIVE`, `ROLL_DRIVE`, `PIVOT_DRIVE` | Joint drive stiffness / damping (tracking accuracy). |
| `PORT_DEPTH_MM`, `PORT_CLEARANCE_MM` | Where the probe starts along the port. |
| `LED_GAIN` | Brightness of the tip camera image. |
| `OBSERVER_*`, `SECOND_VIEWPORT` | Third-person view. |
| `RUN_IMU`, `ROS_*`, `GT_FRAME`, topic names | IMU and ROS 2 output. |
| `REOPEN_ENGINE` | Reopen the engine every run (clean start) or keep the open stage. |

## 11. Limitations and next steps

- **Collisions:** the probe does not physically touch the engine; it follows the commanded motion even through a
  blade. The clearance check warns before the run.
- **Cable sag:** gravity is not applied. Next step: sag from the bench-validated stiffness model, applied as
  kinematic targets.
- **Rotor rotation:** the rotor is static. Next step: rotate it for blade-by-blade inspection.
- **Inspection profiles:** test further trajectories and profiles to evaluate reach, clearance, feasibility and
  image coverage.
- **Optimization / closed loop:** feed μ*(t) from the trajectory optimization, and close the loop with the
  controller over ROS 2.
- **IMU realism:** bias / random-walk models, sim clock, camera–IMU time synchronization.
- **Start-up time:** a single-pose evaluator in the kinematic model would remove the ~1 min μ(t) recovery.

## 12. Troubleshooting

| Message / symptom | Cause and fix |
|---|---|
| `No module named borescope_config` (or any project module) | `SCRIPT_DIR` in `run_inspection.py` does not point to the folder with the files. |
| `no /root/Port_HPC_cutter in the stage` | Engine not loaded: check `ENGINE_USD` (file next to the config) or set `REOPEN_ENGINE = True`. |
| `PhysX tensor API failing here ... switching to USD drive targets` | Normal on some machines; the run continues with USD targets. `BACKEND = "usd"` skips the attempt. |
| Tip camera image very dark | Raise `LED_GAIN`, or Lighting > Stage Lights in the main viewport. |
| Observer window black | Lighting > Camera Light in the "Probe observer" window. |
| `clearance ... WARNING` | The planned motion comes closer to the engine than `CLEARANCE_WARN_MM`: change the profile. |
| `[accel] ... limit reached, clipped` | The motion exceeds `ACCEL_BOUNDS`: the motion is limited at that point. |
| No ROS 2 topics | ROS 2 bridge not enabled in Isaac Sim, or `RUN_IMU = False`. |
| Errors after copying files to a network share | Check that the copied `.py` files are not 0 KB (incomplete copy, e.g. disk full). |
